---
import BaseLayout from "../../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
import MetaPill from "../../components/ui/MetaPill.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method and Publication Policy | FTFN"
  description="How FTFN treats sources, evidence, corrections, launch candidates, and publication readiness."
>
  <section class="page-section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Method</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">Trust is part of the stack.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      FTFN tracks frontier systems, but it does not treat every claim the same.
      The method separates evidence, interpretation, source incentives, and
      local limits before a record can become public intelligence.
    </p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <MetaPill label="No automated publishing" tone="urgency" />
      <MetaPill label="Launch candidate is not Published" tone="review" />
      <MetaPill label="Source limits stay visible" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section status-panel" aria-label="Publication gate">
    <article class="status-item">
      <h2>1. Source</h2>
      <p>Start with official, primary, institutional, or clearly labeled interested-party sources.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="status-item">
      <h2>2. Review</h2>
      <p>Classify evidence quality, maturity, dependencies, constraints, and unresolved gaps.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="status-item">
      <h2>3. Publish</h2>
      <p>Publish only after final copy, citation, caveat, metadata, and correction-policy checks.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section two-column-section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Source Freshness</h2>
      <p>
        FTFN now generates a source monitor from the Atlas source records. It
        compares each source's checked date, update cadence, and capture
        priority to identify which sources are current, which should be watched,
        and which are due for manual review.
      </p>
      <a class="inline-link" href="/atlas/source-monitor/">Open source monitor</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Self-Updating Boundary</h2>
      <p>
        The monitor can update whenever the static site rebuilds, but it does
        not fetch sources, rewrite records, or publish automatically. It is a
        control surface for editorial review, not an ingestion-to-public path.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section two-column-section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Publication Policy</h2>
      <p>
        `Draft Sample`, `Draft`, and `In Review` are not public approval states.
        A launch candidate is only a record selected for final review. `Published`
        is reserved for records with visible sources, current checked dates,
        defensible evidence labels, and a correction path.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a source changes or a claim becomes stale, FTFN should move the record
        to `Needs Update` before rewriting it. If a record is no longer current
        intelligence, it should be archived rather than quietly left to drift.
      </p>
      <a class="inline-link" href="/signals/">Review current signal states</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Source Transparency</h2>
      <p>
        Government data, standards, research findings, company announcements,
        forecasts, and reporting carry different evidentiary weight. Company
        claims remain interested-party evidence until supported by official,
        local, customer, or independent context.
      </p>
      <p>
        Local outcomes require local evidence. A national program, climate
        forecast, or company milestone can shape a local system, but it does not
        prove capacity, readiness, financing, permitting, or acceptance by itself.
      </p>
      <a class="inline-link" href="/atlas/sources/">Open source atlas</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose">
    <h2>Correction And Update Policy</h2>
    <p>
      FTFN should correct or update records when a source changes, a dataset is
      revised, a link breaks, a claim was too strong, a better primary source
      becomes available, or local evidence changes the interpretation.
    </p>
    <p>
      For MVP, material changes are controlled through editorial notes, source
      checked dates, work packages, and the decision log. A public update history
      can become a later feature once records are actually published.
    </p>

    <h2>What FTFN Will Not Do At MVP</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No automated publishing.</li>
      <li>No ingestion-to-public path.</li>
      <li>No numeric 42/59 scoring.</li>
      <li>No local outcome claims without local evidence.</li>
      <li>No company press release treated as deployment proof by itself.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Launch Gate</h2>
    <p>
      The first public launch should be small: a clear homepage, this method
      surface, a few final-reviewed signals, source profiles, caveated local
      system profiles, qualitative dependency maps, and no unsupported claims.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
